what is thread :

if java program is recipe , and jvm is kitchen . normally one chef ( main thread) follows the racipe top to bottom - one step at a time in order

thread is like hiring another chef who can work on a different part of the racipe at the same time, in same kitchen( same program, same memory ) .

process vs thread :

process = a separate kitchen entirly ( it's own memory , it's own resources ).
ex - chorme and intellij different process

thread = a chef working inside the same kitchen . multiple threads share same memory ( heap and static variable) but each has it's own call stack.

shared memory part is single most important face in multithreading - it's reason both the power ( fast , parellel work) and pain ( bugs , race condition ) exist.

why do we need multiple threads :

1. concurrency : dealing with multiple things at once
managing multiple tasks overlapping in time
best for i/o bound task - waiting for databases or apis
ex. server handling 1000 client requests on a single - core machine by switching between them rapidly.

2. parallelism : doing many things at once actually
executing multiple operations at the exact same instant
best for cpu bound task - video rendering or data processing
ex. using 8 cpu cores to sum 4 differnet chunks of an array simultaneusly.


like to understand-  running an async web application on an single core cpu computer . the computer rapidly switches between processing user input and fetching data

but when we run the async app on modern 8 core cpu, the program structure allows multiple requests to be porcessed and the physical hardware allows those request to be executed across the different cores

single core machiene can have concurrency but not parellelism
but multicore can have both



