real life scenirio - file downloader

thread 1 ( main thread ) - keeps the app responsive - you can still click buttons , scroll and cancle

thread 2 ( background thread ) - downlaod the file which takse time

if everything ran on one thread , clicking on cancle button wouldn't even register untill the download finished - beacouse the single chef is still busy follwoing the recipe and can't stop to listen to you.

another conceret one : a web server when 100 user hit your wbesite at once the serber doesnt create 100 processes - it typically hands over each request to a separate thread from a thread pool , so request are handled concurrently instead of one user waiting for the previos user's request to fully finished

behindd the scene -
each thread has its own stack but shared the heap
local variable -> live on the thread's own stack -> not shared no concflict
objects created with new static varible -> live on the heap -> shared across all the threads this is excatly why two threads modifiying the same object can step on each other

os does the actual scheduling
an java thread maps to an os level thread (in modern jvms histocraclly this wasnt always true - green threads existed but ignore that for now)
the os schedular decided which thread runs on which core
and for how long using time slicing
you dont control the exact order this is why multithreading bugs are often non-determinsitc ( works 99 time fails on 100th )

context switching has a cost
when cpu sitches from running thread a to thread b it has a save thread a's state and load thread b's state
too many threads competing for too few cores wasted time context switching isntead of doing real work
so more thread is not automatically faster

jvm's main thread
